---
// Lesson 10.1f — 2-Button Jam: Showcase & Retro
// rpg jam day: arcade-style showcase + structured retro.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.1f — 2-Button Jam: Showcase &amp; Retro</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Six days ends here: the room becomes an arcade for half the
    class, then tells the truth about itself for the other half.
    Ship, show, celebrate — then retro like engineers, because the
    point of the jam was never the game; it was discovering what
    you can do with two buttons and a deadline.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Showcase</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Format: the arcade row.</strong> Games on machines
    around the room, sticky pitch by each keyboard. Two modes,
    alternating:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>As a player:</strong> play at least four games.
            For each, one star vote: BEST FEEL (which two buttons
            argued best?), BEST IDEA, BEST JUICE (which feedback
            taught fastest?).</li>
        <li><strong>As a host:</strong> stand by your game only to
            fix CRASHES. Watch first presses without explaining.
            Count silently: how many players smiled
            (<a href="10.1d.html">10.1d</a> smile-omics), how many
            read the pitch sticky, how many stayed past 60
            seconds.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Every team is host and player in equal measure — the jam
    community's contract.</p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Retro</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The retro board — all four quadrants, team by team,
    3 minutes each, honest:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Went well:</strong> one process fact, not a
            feeling — "the sticky-note risk got cut at 15 min and
            saved the game"</li>
        <li><strong>Went wrong:</strong> same honesty — "we tuned
            feel on Day 1 before the loop existed" (<a href="10.1b.html">10.1b</a>
            rule, learned the hard way)</li>
        <li><strong>The two-button discovery:</strong> what did
            the CONSTRAINT give you that free choice would not
            have? (Every jam has one — name yours.)</li>
        <li><strong>Next jam (<a href="10.2a.html">10.2a</a>):
            one rule you adopt.</strong> From this class's
            combined retro notes: which process fix is universal?</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Archive it</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Tag the release: <code>git tag jam-2button &amp;&amp; git push --tags</code>.</li>
            <li>Add RETRO.md to the repo: the four quadrants, your
                star counts, and the smile numbers. Future-you
                (starting the card jam,
                <a href="10.2a.html">10.2a</a>) reads this
                first.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Reflect: the constraint's receipt</h3>
        <p>
            What did limiting your entire game to two buttons give your
            design that free choice would not have — and which single
            decision this week made the constraint feel like a gift
            rather than a cage?
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Show the build, host silently, and take the smile/count data</li>
            <li>Give one honest "went wrong" and one "went well" with evidence</li>
            <li>Name your constraint discovery in one sentence</li>
            <li>Leave a tagged repo + RETRO.md as the jam's artifact</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season</a> — showcase + retro formats</li>
        <li><a href="../projects.html">Projects</a> — your work, collected</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.2a.html">10.2a — Card Jam Kickoff: Lists &amp; Arrays</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="10.1f" title="10.1f 2-Button Jam: Showcase &amp; Retro — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
